An Echo Fragment is a piece of something that no longer exists, embodied well enough to be carried. Holding one gives what it holds and burns away the rest, so a bearer needs power enough to survive it; the fragment tears the whole of that bearer's life out of the world's record, and no one can say who they were. What stands three times removed from a life is not counted part of it, and survives the tearing.

The [[Divinity/Althane/The Stranger|Stranger]] held one at the centre of [[Geography/Althane/index|Althane]]. He took it from its previous holder, [[People/Althane/Karen Sill|Karen Sill]], laying a hand on the bound man's face. An echo of a bearer's life can be watched, and by it the name the fragment tore away may be learned. The Weft killed the Stranger and gave his fragment to [[People/Althane/The Broker|T3C]].

[[People/Althane/The Broker|T3C of Sedecium]] hunted it, holding that its removal changes nothing for the world. He took it up. It gave him the one thing they had wanted all along: it made him a god, and it tore him out of every record, so that no one remembers him. The pavilion of statues at [[Org/Sedecium|Sedecium]] counts three where it once counted four.

There are three. When [[Divinity/Formless|Formless]], the Higher Kind of Old Thessia, gave itself up to make the [[Divinity/Thessians|Thessians]], the universe rushed in to fill the space it left, and three waves of the rebound formed links with the missing god. An unprepared holder is rooted in place, unable to move or to give the fragment up. A holder gains some of Formless's powers: immense, godly power and the knowledge to wield it, and a blanket anonymity. That anonymity is why the Weft never knew the name of the one at the centre of Althane and called him the Feep.
